/**

@page page_exahype2_numerics_ADER-DG ADER-DG

\tableofcontents

The ADER-DG (Discontinuous Galerkin with Arbitrary high-order derivative time stepping) method is the main numerical
approach used in ExaHyPE. It is a high-order method both in space and time, and requires lower communication compared to the DG method. Here, we provide an
overview of the method; for more details, see <a href="https://www.mdpi.com/2075-1680/7/3/63">the ADER-DG overview paper</a>.

Each timestep of the ADER-DG algorithm consists of two key steps:
1. a <em> predictor step </em>, which solves a space-time DG problem locally within each cell
2. a <em> corrector step </em>, which uses the results of the predictor to compute the solution at the next timestep via a
discontinuous Galerkin scheme in space, and considering the influence of neighbour elements

For simplicity, we consider the problem \f$ \frac{\partial Q}{\partial t} + \nabla \cdot F(Q) = 0. \f$
The spatial domain \f$ \Omega \f$ is discretized on a tree-structured Cartesian grid of cells \f$ T_i \f$. At each timestep \f$ t^n \f$,
the numerical solution \f$ Q_h \f$ is then represented within each cell \f$ T_i \f$ by piecewise polynomials of maximum degree \f$ N \geq 0 \f$:
\f[
    Q_h(x, t^n) = \sum_{l}\hat{Q}_l^n \phi_l(x)
\f]
with the degrees of freedom \f$ \hat{Q}_l^n \f$

### Predictor
The job of the predictor is to provide an estimate for each element of the space-time solution until the next timestep. Following the usual Galerkin approach, but for a space-time element \f$ T_i \times [t^n, t^{n+1}]\f$, we multiply
our problem with space-time test function \f$ \theta_k(x,t) \f$ and integrate in space and time to obtain
\f[
    \int_{\Delta_t} \int_{T_i} \theta_k \frac{\partial q_h}{\partial t} dxdt + \int_{\Delta_t} \int_{T_i} \theta_k \nabla \cdot F(q_h) dxdt = 0
\f]
We introduce the discrete space-time solution \f$ q_h \f$ and flux \f$ F_h \f$:
\f[
    q_h(x,t) = \sum_l \hat{q_l}\theta_l(x,t) \text{ and } F_h(x,t) = \sum_l \hat{F_l}\theta_l(x,t)
\f]

By integration by parts, and substitution of the discrete space-time solution and fluxes, we yield
\f[
    \left( \int_{T_i} \theta_k(x, t^{n+1}) \theta_l(x, t^{n+1}) dxdt - \int_{\Delta_t }\int_{T_i} \frac{\partial \theta_k}{\partial t} \theta_l dxdt \right) \hat{q}_l + \left( \int_{\Delta_t }\int_{T_i} \theta_k \nabla \theta_l dxdt \right) F(\hat{q}_l) = \left( \int_{T_i} \theta_k(x, t^n)\phi_l dx \right) \hat{Q}_l^n
\f]
Note that the Einstein's summation convention is used here. In ExaHyPE this is solved in general via Picard iterations. And for linear PDEs, we use the Cauchy-Kowalevskaya method to lower
computational cost.

### Corrector

The corrector builds on the cell-local space-time solutions \f$ q_h(x,t)\f$ to compute a high-order-accurate solution at
timestep \f$ t^{n+1} \f$.
We first discretize our PDE in space, following the Galerkin approach, and in addition, integrate over \f$ \Delta_t = [t^n, t^{n+1}] \f$:
\f[
    \int_{\Delta_t} \int_{T_i} \theta_k(x) \frac{\partial Q_h}{\partial t} dxdt + \int_{\Delta_t} \int_{T_i} \theta_k \nabla \cdot F(Q_h) dxdt = 0
\f]
By integration by parts of the second term, we get

\f[
    \int_{\Delta_t} \int_{T_i} \theta_k(x) \frac{\partial Q_h}{\partial t} dxdt + \int_{\Delta_t} \int_{\partial T_i} \theta_k  F(Q_h) \cdot n  dSdt  - \int_{\Delta_t} \int_{T_i} \nabla \theta_k \cdot F(Q_h) dxdt= 0
\f]
where \f$ n \f$ is the outward pointing unit normal vector on the surface \f$ \partial T_i \f$.
For the first term, we integrate over \f$ \frac{\partial Q_h}{\partial t}\f$; for the second term, we require a numerical flux function \f$ G \f$ as in DG schemes. We thus obtain
\f[
    \left(\int_{T_i} \phi_k \phi_l dx \right)(\hat{Q}_l^{n+1} - \hat{Q}_l^n) + \int_{\Delta_t }\int_{T_i} \phi_k G(q_h^-, q_h^+)\cdot n dSdt - \int_{\Delta_t }\int_{T_i} \nabla \phi_k \cdot F(q_h)dxdt = 0
\f]


*/
